window.BanesDocs={"atlas": [], "chronicle": [{"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>The mushroom-gatherer</h2>\n<p>Marigold Cotton had crossed the Brandywine for mushrooms. Her children were grown and settled about the\nShire, her husband was dead, and she had taken to going further afield than a respectable hobbit\nought. Even so, the river had always been a boundary to her. She had never crossed it before.</p>\n<p>The Old Forest smelled different from anything on the Shire side. A garden smells one way and a\ncommon another, and the woods of the Shire different again; but this was older than any\nof them, a thick musky smell of loam and leaf-rot and wet bark. She found a few mushrooms quickly,\nand then more, and many were kinds she had never seen. She kept a sense of the way back. She also\nwent in deeper than she had meant to.</p>\n<p>Then she heard singing. It was a man's voice, a deep baritone, loud without bellowing, and it filled\nthe trees. It came from a brook nearby, and the song seemed to be about fish.</p>\n<p>Marigold went towards it quietly, as only a hobbit can. The soil was soft under her bare feet. At the brook stood a big man — big even for one of the Big Folk, some six feet and more, and\nslim — skipping stones down the water. He was very good at it. The stones went in long low arcs and\nbounced four and five times. She watched until he threw a particularly fine one, and then she\ncould not help herself.</p>\n<p>He turned at once and swept her a grand bow. \"I didn't know I had an audience! Come and try your\nhand.\"</p>\n<p>She ran down and tried her hand. She kept her apron gathered up, with the morels hidden in it, but\nhe sniffed the air hugely and smiled as if he knew exactly what was there.</p>\n<p>\"The south side of the oaks is where the best mushrooms are,\" he said. \"Goldberry is making some\nfor supper tonight. Will you join us? The name's Tom. What's yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"Marigold.\"</p>\n<p>\"Marigold! Wonderful.\"</p>\n<p>He led her through the wood, singing again. This song was in a language she did not know. It was\nbeautiful, and a little sad in a way she could not place. They came to a small cottage with\nanimals wandering about it, and Tom burst in at the door.</p>\n<p>\"Goldberry! I've brought a guest for supper!\"</p>\n<p>A fair-haired woman stood over a great pot on the fire. She welcomed Marigold as though she had\nbeen expected, and there were bowls already poured.</p>\n<h2>The Elf and the bear</h2>\n<p>Makheneb was in the Old Forest too, for his own reasons.</p>\n<p>He was an Elf of Mirkwood, a Warden of that wood, young for his people at something under three\nhundred years. He had fought in the Battle of Five Armies. For the last while his luck had been\nbad; his family had fallen on hard times long ago, and lately he had lost nearly everything he\ncarried, down to the shirt on his back. He had come west on foot and lived off the land.</p>\n<p>What drew him was a feeling. Once or twice in his long life he had been near something truly evil\n— near wraiths, or things like them — and each time there had been the same\nprickle down the spine, the same awareness of a presence close by. What he felt now was not that.\nIt lacked that depth of malice. But it was alien, and it had a pull to it, and for some time it had\ndrawn him westward without telling him why.</p>\n<p>He cast about the forest for its source and found nothing he could follow. But he knew where he\nwas. He had heard his elders speak of this country as Tom's land, and they spoke of Tom as older\nthan themselves, and not an Elf, and that had always puzzled him. They did not speak of Tom with\nawe. They mentioned him in passing, the way one mentions the weather: <em>and then the thing went off\ninto Tom's land</em>, and that was the end of the story.</p>\n<p>As the light began to fail he heard a woman singing, far off, and a man's voice joined hers. He\nstood listening. Behind him something snuffled.</p>\n<p>It was a bear, something like a black bear, and much too close. Makheneb held his bow ready and\nmade no threatening move, and began to back away slowly towards the singing. The bear came on. He\ndid not trust his feet in the gloom, and the first blow nearly caught him. He began to run between\nthe trees, weaving from trunk to trunk to keep them between himself and the bear, and blows that\nwould have taken his face off shuddered into bark instead.</p>\n<p>Then the song he had been following broke off. A little later a new one began, closer: a low\nvoice, singing a slow and soothing song in a language Makheneb did not know. The bear pulled up.\nIt sat back on its haunches with its forepaws in front of it and stared at him sullenly.</p>\n<p>The song had a refrain that came round and round. Makheneb caught it and began to sing it too. The\nbear did not seem to mind.</p>\n<p>Out of the dusk came a tall man in bright yellow boots. He laughed, scratched the bear behind the\nears, and rubbed its head. \"Be off with you,\" he said, and the bear stumbled away into the trees.\nThen he looked Makheneb up and down.</p>\n<p>\"An Elf! Welcome, welcome to Tom's land! I'm Tom.\"</p>\n<p>\"I have heard much and little of you,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>\"Well then, come and have supper.\"</p>\n<p>\"I have not had a good meal in quite some time.\"</p>\n<h2>The runaway pony</h2>\n<p>Lily Goatleaf had left Bree in a hurry. She waited tables at the Prancing Pony, and over the years\nshe had listened closely to the Rangers when they trusted someone enough to talk. She had\nlearned from them that Bree-land was not the safe place Bree-folk took it for. It was a small\nisland with enemies all round it that no one else could see. She had never told anyone. They would\nnot have believed her.</p>\n<p>Lately the folk coming through Bree had grown stranger and more unsavoury, and they were gathering\nthere. Then a Ranger called Findemir passed through the inn. She overheard him, speaking low, and\nlearned that he was setting out west; and he was gone again before she could speak with him. She\nfelt a dire portent in it, and decided she could not wait any longer. She borrowed a half-starved pony called\nBuddy on the vague promise that she needed to leave town for a while, and went after the Ranger to\nbring back help.</p>\n<p>Perry Oakroot saw her go and came with her: not to bring her back, but to help. They were distant cousins, of a kind that depends on which way you\ncount. Perry was a Man of Bree and one of the gate-wardens of Bree-land, and he had spent many cold\nlonely nights on watch at the gates with only the stars and the owls for company. He knew a good\nmany people in town, and a good many travellers by sight. He had come to know some of them as good\nfolk, not at all like Bill Ferny and his cronies. He had not been free from fear or care for some\ntime.</p>\n<p>They were a day out of Bree on the East Road, walking and leading Buddy with their gear on his\nback. Lily hoped Findemir had at least stopped in Hobbiton, and that someone at an inn there\ncould tell them where he had gone. Dusk was coming on and they were talking about where to camp\nwhen something frightened Buddy. He bolted off the road and down into the forest, and everything\nthey owned went with him.</p>\n<p>Lily sprinted after him at once. Perry stood in the road for a moment. He was thinking of Butterbur,\nand what Butterbur would say. Then he ran after her. The ground fell away steeply from the road. Lily was quick\nand sure-footed, and she overtook the pony a little way into the trees and began to calm him.\nPerry came down the slope with more speed than he had meant to. At the first stand of trees a root\ncaught his foot and he fell flat on his face.</p>\n<p>He got up at once, took his bearings, and went on at a measured jog, watching the ground for roots\nthe whole way.</p>\n<p>While Lily soothed Buddy, another horse came walking towards them out of the trees. It had no\ntether and it was plainly well cared for, and it came up to them without any fear at all. It\nseemed to be hoping for a treat. Lily edged towards it carefully, as one approaches a new friend,\nand it let her.</p>\n<p>Perry stumbled out of the wood covered in twigs, his face smeared with dirt. \"What happened, Lily?\"</p>\n<p>Lily was already rummaging in the pack for an apple. She gave Buddy one and offered another to the\nstrange horse, but it turned and cantered off.</p>\n<p>\"We should be careful,\" said Perry. \"There are strange folk in these parts.\"</p>\n<p>\"Strange folk can't have horses?\"</p>\n<p>\"We should follow it. It may even be the Ranger's. But carefully.\"</p>\n<p>They followed. They passed through a garden, though it was not like any garden in Bree. Nothing was\nfenced or set in rows. The vegetables tumbled over one another, and the beans zigzagged all the way\nup to a cottage door. As they came round the corner of the house they saw a big man in bright\nyellow boots and a blue jacket stroking the horse's mane and feeding it something.</p>\n<p>Lily called out to him. The man turned to the horse. \"Strawberry, you've brought new friends! And\nwho's this?\" He held out his hand, and there was an apple in it that had not been there before,\nand Buddy took it.</p>\n<p>\"This is Buddy,\" said Lily. \"I've only had him a day, and I would do anything for him.\"</p>\n<p>\"Anything for Buddy,\" said Perry. \"What is your name, sir?\"</p>\n<p>\"Tom. Tom, and yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"Perry.\"</p>\n<p>\"Lily.\"</p>\n<p>\"Perry and Lily! Wonderful. Well, you must stay for supper. We have a mushroom stew, and the peaches\nhave come in, and there are wild strawberries not far off — hence the horse. I go and gather them\nnow and then.\" He promised that Buddy would have plenty to eat. Lily told him they had been trying\nto fatten Buddy up the whole way. It had been their pet project.</p>\n<h2>Supper</h2>\n<p>Tom brought them in and introduced his wife, Goldberry, and she sat them down beside Marigold.\nPerry had seen hobbits at the Prancing Pony, which kept rooms for them, and dealt with a few at the\ngate, but he could count on one hand the hobbits he had ever spoken to. Lily introduced herself.\nMarigold was glad to see young people, and before long she had brought out her mushrooms and was\nteaching Lily their names. Lily listened. She liked learning things. But the Ranger was never far\nfrom her mind.</p>\n<p>Tom and Goldberry were perfect hosts, and nothing seemed to trouble them. Their land was bountiful,\nbut they did not seem to work it hard or to fret over it. Animals wandered in and out of the house\nas they pleased. Nothing was penned and nothing was tied. Strawberry ambled where she liked. There\nwas a pumpkin patch in one corner that made no sense where it was.</p>\n<p>After supper Tom asked, \"You don't mind if we sing a little, do you?\"</p>\n<p>Lily said she would love it. They built up the fire and served tea and small cakes, and Tom and\nGoldberry took turns singing. Some of the songs were in languages the guests knew and some were\nnot. The words they understood were mostly tender things about the land: a walk on a warm spring\nday in the rain, and how the rain felt; the moment when the sun catches a stream just so. But now\nand then a verse spoke of the land in ways that did not match anything they knew of it, or named\npeople and places none of them had ever heard of.</p>\n<p>In the middle of a duet Tom stopped.</p>\n<p>\"Sorry, my love. The bears are getting a little anxious. I'll be right back.\" And he walked out of\nthe door.</p>\n<p>Perry asked whether there was danger, and whether Tom might want help.</p>\n<p>\"We don't usually have trouble with the bears,\" said Goldberry. \"But sometimes travellers meet\nthem, and then there can be trouble. Tom likes to make sure nobody gets too ornery. I wouldn't fancy\nyour chances if you came to fisticuffs with a bear.\" Then she said, \"I expect we'll have another\nguest. I'll warm up some supper. Would one of you set a place, and fetch some wine?\"</p>\n<p>They set a place. Before long Tom came back with Makheneb behind him.</p>\n<p>\"I knew to expect somebody,\" Tom said. \"I must admit I wasn't quite expecting you lot. We'll talk\nonce you've finished. Eat! Sit! Enjoy yourselves!\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb ate like one who had not eaten for some time. Now and then he glanced round the room. Tom\nsettled by the fire with a pipe whose bowl was nearly the size of a small dish, and offered a pipe\nto anyone who wanted one. His mood had grown quieter. Goldberry got out her knitting, put it away\nagain, and went about tidying.</p>\n<p>\"There are not many of your people outside the Shire,\" Makheneb said to Marigold.</p>\n<p>\"You'd be surprised how many hobbits love to see the world,\" said Marigold. \"Nothing surprises me\nout here.\" She had never seen an Elf before. On the inside she was wondering what to do, and whether\nshe had already offended him.</p>\n<p>He had not seen many hobbits either. There were few in Mirkwood; one, in living memory. But he had\nbeen at the Battle of Five Armies, and he knew the tale of the hobbit who had smuggled the Dwarves\nout of the Elvenking's halls.</p>\n<p>\"What brings two Men of Bree out here?\" he asked.</p>\n<p>\"Is it so plain that we're from Bree?\"</p>\n<p>\"I can smell it.\"</p>\n<p>\"Chasing a pony,\" said Perry. \"And what brings an Elf of Mirkwood this far west?\"</p>\n<p>\"A feeling,\" said Makheneb. \"Not good, and not bad. Strange.\"</p>\n<p>\"The Shadow grows in the East,\" said Marigold.</p>\n<p>\"And he's come west,\" said Perry.</p>\n<p>\"Perhaps the Shadow has,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>Perry said they had felt something too. Unsavoury folk were gathering in Bree, and Bree needed\nhelp. Lily turned to Tom and Goldberry and described the Ranger they were chasing. She said he was\ntall, by which she meant anyone taller than a Bree-lander.</p>\n<p>Goldberry knew him at once. \"Oh — Findemir! He came through only the other day.\" She described him\nbetter than Lily had: a bushy, curly auburn beard, no moustache, and hair that curled too, though\nnot tightly.</p>\n<p>\"That's why you're here,\" said Tom. \"That makes perfect sense. That is why you're here.\"</p>\n<p>Then he said, \"You aren't wrong to feel there are ill winds about. It seems things have been loosed\nthat need to be put back to sleep. Findemir was going north, beyond Fornost, to Barad Tarminalë. He is looking for\nhelp. The Rangers are dealing with strange creatures coming through the woods, and he believes some\nold power has loosed them. They have started coming into the Old Forest. I've had to kick one or two\nof them in the hind parts.\"</p>\n<p>\"What do they look like?\"</p>\n<p>\"The one I met had the form of a wolf. It was a good deal bigger than most wolves, and it was\nalone, which is odd in a wolf. Its fur was white and greyish. And though we are having a lovely\nspring here, it carried the hoar-frost of winter with it.\"</p>\n<p>\"So the one we're looking for is looking for help with strange creatures,\" said Marigold. \"And we\nare that help?\"</p>\n<p>\"Within the Old Forest my power is complete,\" said Tom. \"Beyond it, I will give whatever aid I can.\nYou have my blessing and my support in all you do, and I would dearly like you to come back and tell\nus what Findemir has found. You have a hobbit in Marigold, an Elf in Makheneb, and two good Men of\nBree. I believe that with you we will get to the bottom of this, and set the wild things right\nagain.\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb had another realisation, once the bear had worn off and his belly was full. This house was\nthe heart of it. The strangeness he had followed west came from here. He had\ntravelled west to find it, and now that he had, he was not glad. He had already been robbed down to\nhis shirt, and the journey had hardly begun. At least, he thought, it had not all been hunger.</p>\n<p>So the four of them became a company, with Tom Bombadil and Goldberry as their patrons, and Tom's\nhouse as a refuge to return to.</p>\n<h2>Freeze-berries</h2>\n<p>The guests slept on bedrolls and cots that night. Marigold and Lily were up before dawn, both of\nthem early risers, and they woke to the smell of something like strawberry muffins. Tom was in the\nkitchen with flour to the elbows and flour on every surface, pulling a tray out of the oven.</p>\n<p>\"Eat them while they're hot!\"</p>\n<p>There were strawberries in them, and another berry none of them knew, with a tartness like\nrhubarb. \"Freeze-berries,\" Tom said. \"They only grow in the Old Forest. They come up just after the\nlast snow is gone, and they're only about for a few weeks.\"</p>\n<p>Goldberry came in an hour later from seeing to the animals, and by then Perry and Makheneb were up.</p>\n<p>Over breakfast somebody asked Lily, quietly, \"Is he a Man?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't think so,\" said Lily. \"But if you asked me anything at all this morning, I'd say I don't\nthink so.\"</p>\n<p>They turned to Makheneb, who as an Elf was presumed to know. \"He is not of my kind. Even the\neldest of my kin know little of him. I half thought he was a tale. But here he is. He seems a good\none to have owe us a favour.\"</p>\n<p>\"The man, the myth, the legend,\" said Tom, coming in at the door. He had plainly heard every word.</p>\n<p>They spread Perry's maps on the table, weighing the corners down with muffins. The maps had come\ndown to him from his grandfather Herbert. Perry asked Tom which way he would go: east along the Road\nand then north to Fornost, which might be quicker, or straight north across country if speed\nmattered. Tom said roads were kinder to ponies. Then he ran a jammy finger over the map and stopped. \"Oh, that won't do. That\nhasn't been there for a couple of generations.\" He rummaged in his pockets for a stub of pencil and\nbegan to mark the map in three places. He did not say what the marks meant.</p>\n<p>There was a jam smudge on them now as well.</p>\n<p>Goldberry led Strawberry over to Makheneb. \"Your companions all have mounts. Your road will go more\nsmoothly if you have one too. We only ask that you bring Strawberry back to us within a year, when\nyou're done. Care for her as I would my own.\" Lily had Buddy. Perry had brought his old horse, Leaper, who\nhad earned the name long ago and no longer deserved it. Marigold had a pony too, Margarita. All of\nthem were a little jealous of Makheneb.</p>\n<p>Goldberry gave them a basket of the mushrooms left over from supper, some of them kinds even\nMarigold had not seen, and a few jars of preserves. They set out for Barad Tarminalë, ten days'\njourney away, in the spring of the year 2965.</p>\n<h2>The road north</h2>\n<p>They kept to the road. Not far along it, riding in the sun on a fine spring day, they saw the bear.</p>\n<p>It had seen them too, and it was ambling towards the road on a line that would meet them. Makheneb\nbegan to sing the song Tom had sung to it. He got one verse out before he told the others to join\nin, and they did, though none of them knew what the words meant:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Bârun, Bârun, zîrân lô,</em><br />\n<em>Uzûn bâri nardim tô.</em><br />\n<em>Katha lô îdô kalab,</em><br />\n<em>Nardê batîr, nardê khâlab.</em><br />\n<em>Zimra-nîdô, azra-lad,</em><br />\n<em>Aphadad, lô nardad!</em><br />\n<em>Nimmê, Bârun, târik bal,</em><br />\n<em>Anadê thâni, nardê zal.</em> The bear came up to the road and fell in\nbeside Strawberry, and walked along with them for two hours. Then it seemed to grow bored and\nwandered off another way.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>When the song died down and they had wet their throats, the questions came. \"Are you friends?\"</p>\n<p>\"It is an Elf thing,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>\"Now you know what to do if you meet a bear,\" said Makheneb. \"I would advise exactly that.\"</p>\n<p>One of them tried the song alone a little later. It did not go well.</p>\n<p>\"Maybe it only works when we sing it together,\" said another. \"I think we could make a drinking\nsong of it.\"</p>\n<p>They passed west of Bree at the crossroads and went north up the old road without going into the\ntown. Perry was relieved. He had not asked for much leave from the gate, and he did not care to explain\nthe whole adventure to everyone in town. Thanks to Tom and Goldberry they had all the\nfood they needed.</p>\n<p>Three days or so out, the road began to change. The roads east and west and south of Bree were kept\nup well enough, but the North had not been a place of trade for a long time, and on this road they\nmet no one at all. Lily scouted ahead, riding forward and falling back to rejoin the others. On one\nof her rides she came to a place where the road had been swept away. For a few hundred feet it was\nsimply gone: shoved up, broken, and pushed under the earth.</p>\n<p>Her first feeling was awe at whatever had had the strength to do that. Her second was dread,\nbecause it did not look like the work of wind or flood. It looked as if someone had done it. They\npicked their way round it with care, and it wearied them.</p>\n<p>After that the going was better. They found a path through the foothills that no one seemed to\nuse any longer. It had been well trodden once, and no trees had grown up in it. They followed it and\nmade surprisingly good time.</p>\n<h2>The woman in the foothills</h2>\n<p>In the foothills Makheneb went off from the path, drawn by some smell or some sense of wrongness,\nand found the dead.</p>\n<p>There were four of them, mostly bone, and long dead. Three were Orcs or goblins of some kind. The\nfourth had been an explorer, well equipped for the Wild and nothing like a travelling merchant. Her\nbody was much of a size with his own, and her helm and shield were still sound. He had gone\nwithout both since his luck turned, and he took them.</p>\n<p>Makheneb did not want to leave them lying as they were. There were trees enough there, though it\nwas not quite a forest. He gathered what personal things he could find, and then he laid the dead\nto rest in the way of his people. The earth and the growing things closed about them faster than\nany earth should, and the green grew over them. He said a few halting words for them. He\nwished them rest and asked that their fathers might look on them. He felt sure afterwards that no\nwight or wraith would rise from that place.</p>\n<p>While he worked, he saw clearly how she had died. She had been caught off her guard by three or\nfour Orc-warriors. She had killed two of them and maimed a third so badly that he could not move,\nand the others had left him there. Then they had killed her.</p>\n<p>Beyond the foothills Perry's maps ran out. None of them had been this far north, and none of them\nknew the country. Lily was scouting again, and she led them wrong for half a day. The broken road\nstill troubled her. She had been swept up in the adventure — in Tom, and Strawberry, and the new\nfriends — and the broken road had brought her back to why she had set out. It disheartened her, too:\nshe had only just left Bree, and already she was seeing more of the thing she had left to find help\nagainst.</p>\n<p>A day and a half later they saw the shape of hills that Tom had described. Coming in from the west\nby the wrong road turned out to be lucky. The fort was a ring of stone, nearly cut into the hills,\nand it lay mostly on their northern and western faces. From the south, the way they should have\ncome, they might never have seen it.</p>\n<h2>Barad Tarminalë</h2>\n<p>Lily knew more than the others about the Dúnedain. The Rangers had often talked more freely with her\nthan with most folk in Bree, because she was gently curious and polite, never prying, and she had\nthe innkeeper's habit of hospitality. They had told her of their own customs of hospitality. They\nkept way-stations across the wild lands. There was nearly always a steward at such a place,\ntaking turns with others, to keep a fire lit and the house open. You could turn up unannounced,\neven after a month in the wild, and find the plainest of comforts, and help if you were badly\nhurt. She guessed this was such a place. She could not be sure the welcome would extend to people\nwho were not Rangers, but she thought the odds were good.</p>\n<p>\"We should go up openly,\" she said. \"As what we are: tired folk looking for someone. Nothing to\nmake them wary. They'll give us the plain welcome, but they are Rangers, and they're suspicious by\nnature.\"</p>\n<p>They agreed to go up to the gate together and speak from a little way off, so no one inside would\nthink they were rushing it. There was an hour and a half of daylight left. The sun sat just above\nthe hills.</p>\n<p>The gates were great stakes of timber that opened inwards. There was no one on them. Lily went a\nlittle ahead of the others. \"Hello?\" No answer came. She called louder. \"I'm looking for Findemir!\"</p>\n<p>\"One minute!\" a woman's voice called from within.</p>\n<p>The gates creaked. A woman had hold of a big iron ring on one side and was walking it slowly\nbackward. It was a heavy gate, but not a hard one once it was moving. \"Come in, come in,\" she said.\nThen she saw the rest of them and looked startled. \"Well! What a party. Come in and warm\nyourselves.\"</p>\n<p>\"What's your name?\" asked Perry.</p>\n<p>\"I'm Moriel.\"</p>\n<p>\"Moriel. I'm Perry, and I'm pleased to make your acquaintance.\"</p>\n<p>She was a sturdy woman, dressed as a Ranger, in good boots, with one of the heavy cloaks they\nwear against the rain. It was a little much for the season, though the nights were still cold. She\nlooked as if she meant to be on the road before long.</p>\n<p>\"You've only just missed Findemir,\" she said. \"But I can help you a little. Come and eat something,\nand let me get you settled.\" She shut the gate behind them and showed them round.</p>\n<p>Inside, the place was all put away. There were cabins with bare beds, and cupboards that were\nmostly empty. Moriel kept a small store in one of them, and she began taking out vegetables for a\nstew. She had eaten already, she said, but they could cook something together. She talked freely,\nand she was curious about everything: how they knew Findemir, and what had brought them all the way\nup here.</p>\n<p>They told her plainly that they were looking for Findemir. Bree needed help, he had seemed a capable\nman when he came through, and he had said he might need help himself in the north. They did not\nmention Tom.</p>\n<p>She was the only one there. She was waiting for someone and would go west in a day or two, when they\narrived. She had kept the winter at this post for some months, and only three travellers had come\nall season. It was her first winter here. At the places she had wintered before there had been\nthirty or forty people, all hunkered down for the cold, and it had been more of a festival. Here\nthere had been a month and a half with no one at all. She had invented a good many games in that\ntime and got very good at beating herself, but she could not promise she would be much of an\nopponent for anyone else. Lily offered to teach her a card game she knew, for two or three or four\nplayers, and Moriel was glad to learn it.</p>\n<p>Findemir had been there only a night, more or less. There was a task of caretaking that had to be\ndone every year, Moriel said, and this year it was his turn. It lay a day and a half's journey\nnorth-east. She had never gone herself, but she knew others had, and she told them what she knew.</p>\n<p>Perry and Lily had heard the word <em>Númenórean</em>. Now and then a Ranger would turn up in Bree with some\nold thing: a piece of pottery, a tool, a dagger. The designs were strange, and the things were\nsometimes said to have strange properties, and the Rangers always said the same thing about them:\nthat no one knew any longer how such things were made. They called them Númenórean. Lily and Perry\nhad never been sure what that meant — only that there had once been a great people in the North, a\nvery long time ago.</p>\n<p>Findemir's errand was to a place like that. There was a ruin of some kind, and it had to be tended,\nlike grounds that must be kept. That was where he had gone.</p>", "played": "2025-05-06", "slug": "01-from-bree-to-toms-house", "title": "From Bree, to Tom’s House, to Barad Tarminalë"}, {"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>Már</h2>\n<p>Már had been sent north without being told why.</p>\n<p>She was a Dwarf of Durin's folk, a hundred and seventeen years old, and among her clan she was the\none they sent for when something dead would not stay dead. In the mountains there are always things\nthat creep into the places where people live, and she had a knack for finding them and putting an\nend to them. That was her work, and she was known for it. So when a Ranger of the North came to her\nclan on some errand of his own, it was Már they told to go with him. Nobody explained what the\nerrand was. The Ranger, Findemir, plainly knew where he was going and what he meant to do there, and\nhe talked to her as though she knew it too. She did not. She went anyway.</p>\n<p>Findemir would not ride. He insisted on going on foot, and never said why, and so they left their\nmounts at the Rangers' post and walked north together into the hills.</p>\n<p>The land there was low and rolling, with no forest to speak of, only stands of trees here and there.\nIt had been settled once and had long since gone wild. Further south the spring was in full flood,\nwith weeds and flowers bursting into bloom along every road; but these hills seemed not to have\nheard of it. The buds were late. Snow still lay in the lee of the slopes and in the valleys where\nthe shade kept it.</p>\n<p>The wolves came on them a few hours out. There were four, and they were not like any wolves Már had\nseen. They were dire wolves, white-furred and huge, and they were cold. The air about them frosted\nas they moved, though it was spring and the day was mild. Each one was like a walking blizzard, and\nthe biggest was bigger than the rest by a good deal.</p>\n<p>Findemir and Már set their backs to one another and stood on their guard. Two wolves went for each\nof them. Már watched for the openings and made the wolves' strikes go wide, and she beat them off,\nbut the fight went against them. It is hard to judge the reach of a wolf when there are two of them\nand they take turns. The great wolf worked its way in on Findemir. Something drew his eye for an\ninstant, and in that instant it was at his throat.</p>\n<p>He went down. Már saw the blood and saw the wolves close in on him. She could have run. Instead she\nwent in over his body to drive them off him, and one of them snapped at her as she came and another\ncaught her by the leg.</p>\n<h2>Four out of the south</h2>\n<p>The Company had left Barad Tarminalë that morning on Moriel's directions, following Findemir. They\nheard the snarling from over a rise, and came over the top of it to find a Dwarf alone among four\nwolves with a man down at her feet.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had an arrow on the string before the others had taken it in. He picked the wolf that had\njust bitten the Dwarf, the next most dangerous after the great one, and loosed. It was a long shot,\nand the arrow from his great two-handed bow went in with a thud they heard from the rise. The wolf\nsquealed. The arrow stood sideways out of its ribs.</p>\n<p>Perry had a bow, though he had not drawn one in earnest in a long while; his arrow went well wide.\nMarigold had not shot anything in twenty years, and hers fell almost at her feet. Then they were\ndown the slope and in among the wolves.</p>\n<p>Perry came in hard and swung his long-hafted axe at one of them. He did not cleave it. He clipped it,\nand the weight of the blade knocked it sideways and left it winded and staggering.</p>\n<p>Lily had grown up on a farm, and all her training with a spear had been mock fights with her cousins\nin the yard. She told herself this was the same thing. It was the biggest and most savage animal she\nhad ever seen, and she was in a panic, and she put her spear through it with both hands and killed it\noutright. When she pulled the spear free the head of it was white with frost. It was so cold that if\nshe had touched her tongue to it, it would have stuck.</p>\n<p>Marigold went for the great wolf with her axe. Her nerve failed her at the last. At home she had had\na dog called Bobo, one of those big Shire dogs that stand as tall as a hobbit and are as long as\nthree or four of them, and he had been a great soft cuddly creature. This thing had a man's blood\nall over its face, but it was shaped like Bobo. She checked for a fraction of a second too long, and\nthe wolf sprang over her and round behind her. She turned with it and caught it with her axe as it\ncame down.</p>\n<p>The wolf Perry had clipped leapt on him. Its claws sank in under his arm, between the leather of his\ncorslet and his body, and there they jammed. It thrashed to wrench its paw free and savaged him while\nit did. Perry was wounded, and worse than that, he was weary. He had loaded himself with far too much\ngear, and he had not known it until now, when the weight of it dragged at every movement.</p>\n<p>Makheneb saw Perry in danger. He spun round and loosed. The arrow went clean through the wolf's head\nand out the other side. Perry went on shaking it for a moment before he understood that what he was\nshaking was dead weight, and then it was only a matter of untangling himself.</p>\n<p>Marigold called out to all of them that these were only dogs; they need not worry. Lily went in\nscreaming beside her. \"Think of it as a haystack!\" She was pretending she was back on the farm.\nWhatever they had told themselves, it put heart into the others. Már, who was in a fury by now,\ncame in with her axe raised high above her head. The blow was slow and plain to see, and the wolf\nshe aimed it at did not move in time. It was the one Makheneb had shot first. The axe came down and\nit lay still.</p>\n<p>Perry went to Findemir and tried to do something for him. He knew nothing about wounds. While he knelt\nthere, the great wolf came for him and clamped its jaws on his arm.</p>\n<p>It had him fast and could not turn to meet anyone else. Makheneb took the shot. To hit a wolf in the\nhead is hard enough; to hit it in the head without harming the arm in its mouth is harder. Perry felt\nthe jaws slacken, and then begin to tighten again, and he tore his arm out; and the jaws snapped shut\non nothing and stayed shut. The arrow had gone into its head.</p>\n<h2>After the wolves</h2>\n<p>The first thing was Findemir. Lily had a healing balm in her pack. She knew nothing about healing\neither, but Makheneb did, and with the balm and his help she cleaned and bound the wounds. They were\nbad. But Findemir was breathing, and he would not die. His eyes flickered. He could not sit up or\nspeak, and his breath came shallow.</p>\n<p>Perry's wound had stopped bleeding. He had packed it with mud. He was not sure whether that was good\nor bad.</p>\n<p>It was not yet noon. Moriel had said the place Findemir was bound for lay a day and a half from\nBarad Tarminalë, and they were only a few hours out. They could be back at the fort in half a day.\nThey agreed quickly: safety first, and introductions after.</p>\n<p>Findemir could not ride, and so Már built a sledge. It lay low to the ground behind a horse, and she\nbent saplings over it so that they sprang and cushioned it as it rocked, and though the ground was\nrough the litter hardly moved. It was very fine work. Perry rode Leaper back.</p>\n<p>They reached the gate of Barad Tarminalë and shouted. \"Good news! We found him! He's alive!\"</p>\n<h2>The evening at the fort</h2>\n<p>Moriel took Findemir in at once and saw to his wounds.</p>\n<p>Már had questions of her own. The others knew Findemir only by hearsay; they had come looking for\nhim. She had been with him, and yet she knew almost as little. He had talked to her about his errand,\nand she had understood hardly any of it. He had needed to find something. There was some kind of\nmission, and she was supposed to be going along with him. Nobody had ever told her why. \"They sent\nme with him and told me nothing,\" she said. \"I do everything for them, and they tell me nothing.\"</p>\n<p>Lily was over the worst of her shock, and she did what she knew how to do. She put her innkeeper's\nmanner back on and made supper, and opened the preserves Goldberry had given them, and passed round\nher flask of liquor. It had been a long day. She told Moriel and Már about Bree and the ill feeling\nthere, and how they had come looking for Findemir because they needed help. She did not mention Tom.\nNone of them did. They did not understand Tom themselves, and they did not know how they would\nexplain him to anyone else.</p>\n<p>Lily set her spear in a pot of boiling water to take the frost out of it. It worked; the frost went.\nBut the frost left something behind. Etched into the metal, like the pattern on frosted glass, was an\nafter-image of it, and it would not come off.</p>\n<p>Perry sat with Moriel and asked her everything she could tell him about treating wounds. He had\nrealised that he was wholly incompetent at it. She told him the mud had not been the right move,\nthough it was not always the wrong one; mostly you should wash it. Perry took a great deal of this in.\nHe had some ideas of his own, such as giving a man who was losing blood some of his own. Moriel said\nthat was a lesson for a good deal later. In a few generations, said Lily, the Oakroots might get\nthere.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had gathered up his arrows after the fight. They were covered in the wolves' blood, and he\nsat and studied them. Long ago, when he was young, he had gone on an expedition into the southern\nforest of Mirkwood while the Necromancer still held Dol Guldur. That was the worst place he had ever\nbeen, and the worst thing he had ever felt. This was worse. The longer he looked at it, the more it\nwas like handling something that burned without heat. He snapped the arrow in his hands and threw it\nin the fire. He said nothing to the others. He did not want to frighten them away.</p>\n<p>Lily and Perry talked by the fire. \"How are you doing?\" she asked him.</p>\n<p>\"You ran in screaming,\" said Perry. \"That was impressive. Though I found the haystack confusing.\"</p>\n<p>\"Thank you,\" said Lily. \"It turns out it was not a haystack.\"</p>\n<p>Perry asked what they made of the Dwarf they had picked up. She had simply fallen in with them, and\nthey with her. She had not had much choice. Perry had been half-conscious on his horse for most of\nthe way back, and had hardly seen her.</p>\n<p>It was a quiet evening, and in the course of it the five of them began, a little, to choose one\nanother. Perry felt he owed Makheneb for the shot that had taken the wolf off him, and he wanted to\nbe in the good graces of a traveller who had seen so much more of the world. Már found herself\nwatching out for Marigold. She was used to being the smallest in any company, and here she was not\nthe smallest. Lily was still worried for Perry, and a little guilty that she had not been able to\nkeep her own cousin from harm. And Marigold took to Lily, who was curious about everything and\nalways caring for somebody, and was up at five in the morning like herself.</p>\n<h2>Moriel's charge</h2>\n<p>Late in the evening Moriel took Marigold aside. She had Findemir's satchel, and she laid out what was\nin it on the table: a book bound in leather, a map, and some other things.</p>\n<p>\"Findemir will need a great deal of care,\" she said. \"I expect I'll be here tending him for a while.\nIt seems he was working on something that can't wait. I know you were attacked, and I don't say this\nlightly. But I need you to finish what he started.\"</p>\n<p>She hesitated. \"This isn't something that's shared outside the Dúnedain. But I trust you, in a way I\ncan't quite explain.\"</p>\n<p>She unrolled the map and went over it with Marigold. They had been heading up into the downs, north\nof here. On the map was something Marigold had never seen marked on any map: ruins, underneath what\nlooked like a natural cavern, inside the cavern itself.</p>\n<p>People speak of the First Age and the Second and the Third, Moriel said; the Third is the present\ncount of years, the last few thousand. These ruins went back to the First Age, and the records about\nthem were very incomplete. Marigold had never heard the word <em>Númenórean</em>. Moriel explained that the\nNúmenóreans were the people who had lived in these lands many thousands of years ago. Some of their\nruins lay underground now. There were whole cities hidden away, with the earth risen over them, and\ncities under the sea.</p>\n<p>This place was a very important one, and it needed tending on a schedule. The journal set out the\ninstructions: how to get in, and what to do once inside. There were two levels. One could be called\nthe outside of the front door, and the other the inside of the house, short of the inner vestibule.\nOn each level there was what Moriel called an artifact, though it was not a single thing. It was part\nof the building, a working piece of it, and it had to be kept whole and clean and working. If it was\nbroken, or had pieces missing, that had to be put right.</p>\n<p>It sounded to Marigold like being asked to go through an old empty warehouse and make sure the lamps\nworked on every floor. The journal was clear enough. Its language was odd: it read as if it were a\ncouple of hundred years old, though the instructions were plainly recent. The map was clear too, and\nit led to the same place they had been making for.</p>\n<p>\"Here,\" Moriel said, and put the whole of it into Marigold's hands. \"Once you're there, it should\ntake you a day at the most.\"</p>\n<p>\"We will carry on this mission,\" said Marigold. \"You won't regret trusting us.\"</p>\n<p>Moriel hugged her.</p>\n<h2>The blizzard</h2>\n<p>Marigold woke the others at five in the morning. \"We've got work to do,\" she said, and spread the map\nout, and told them. It was a big responsibility. It was also, she thought, pretty easy. They could\ncertainly handle it.</p>\n<p>It was the direction they had been going anyway, a day and a half away. Lily said that Tom and\nGoldberry had told them to help Findemir, and this was helping Findemir. They would need to learn a\nsong for wolves, Perry said, next time they saw Tom. Már felt that she owed it to Findemir; she had\nbeen given this task in all but name when she was sent with him.</p>\n<p>Some of it did not quite make sense to her. If this was only a matter of going into a warehouse and\nseeing that the lamps worked, why had her clan sent someone whose work was the dead? The thought did\nnot come into her head.</p>\n<p>They camped the first night. On the second day it grew noticeably colder as they climbed. It was not\na mountain they were climbing, only higher hills, a few of them standing shoulder to shoulder; but by\nthe time they came near the place where the cavern should be, they were riding into a full blizzard.</p>\n<p>They could see very little. It felt unnatural, or at least unseasonable. It might have been only a\nfreak storm. None of them had been this far north before, and none of them knew what was usual here.\nThey were very cold. Everyone had put on every layer they owned, with blankets over the top. Már\nrode behind Makheneb on Strawberry, the sprightliest of their mounts.</p>\n<p>Makheneb asked Marigold whether the journal gave any sense of how urgent this was. It did. The work\nhad to be done under a certain moon, within a month at most, or perhaps half a month. They did not\nknow where they stood in that window. An hour or a day would make no difference, but they could not\ngo away for a couple of weeks and come back.</p>\n<p>Perry was worried that if they pressed on now they would go in the wrong direction. His maps did not\nreach this far north. They agreed to look for shelter, and if there was none, to push on.</p>\n<p>Lily found it. Something told her to go higher, though going higher meant more wind. At the top of\none of the hills there were ruins: a large circle of cobbles, broken up, with a low wall round parts\nof it and a ring of pillars, most of them long since fallen. The wall was about three feet high, and\nbetween the wall and the drifts it made a good break from the wind, with room for a fire. No one had\nbeen there. The snow might have hidden the signs, but there were no other signs at all.</p>\n<p>They sat out the storm there for a few hours. It was not quite nightfall when the blizzard died, and\nthe last of the sun came out, though the valley below was already partly in shadow.</p>\n<h2>The cave</h2>\n<p>Perry kept watch on the way they had come, in case the wolves were tracking them; this was wolf\ncountry. Now that the air had cleared he could see a very long way south, and he could see the\nchange from winter into spring across miles of land.</p>\n<p>Makheneb looked the other way, inward, down into a bowl among the hills. Three of the great wolves\nwere walking in single file out of a patch of shadow. He looked again and saw that the shadow was\nthe mouth of a cavern. The wolves were leaving it. He was several hundred feet above them and a long\nway off, and the wind was blowing from them to him. He followed them with his eyes as they wound up\nbetween two of the far foothills and into a glade, half a mile off and still going. Then they were\ngone.</p>\n<p>He climbed down and told the others. \"Three of the big ones. They were leaving.\"</p>\n<p>The place matched the documents. It was the only cave they had seen.</p>\n<p>\"I wouldn't trust them to stay gone,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>The journal said the work had last been done about forty years ago. It said nothing about wolves. But\na great deal can happen in forty years.</p>\n<p>They decided to scout it while the light held. The entrance was wide, perhaps forty feet across, with\na low overhang of frozen snow across the front of it, so that from outside it looked like a low, wide\nslot. Inside, the rock went up in a great arch, thirty feet high or more. Már and Makheneb stayed\noutside to keep watch, and the other three crept in. Perry had so many pots and pans hung about him\nthat he rang like a tinker's cart, and somebody had to reach over and still them.</p>\n<p>At first it was a cave like any other, with earth and pebbles underfoot, and bones, and scat. By the\nlight of Perry's lantern they could see one side where the wolves plainly lay up and slept, though\nnone were there now. Then the cave began to wind, curving slowly round to the right and sloping\ngently down. About the length of a great hall in, the floor under their feet turned to cobbles. The\ncave was natural, but this was not.</p>\n<p>They could not see where it ended. They went back for the others.</p>\n<h2>A night of traps</h2>\n<p>There was a long argument. If they went down now, they might finish before the wolves came back, but\nthey would be hurrying, and they would have to come out the same way, perhaps into the wolves, and\nperhaps hurt. If they waited, they could learn the wolves' habits, but that would waste time. And\nhad the wolves only been passing through? The den inside said otherwise. It was not one day's worth\nof bones.</p>\n<p>In the end they settled on traps. They would give it one night at most. If the wolves came back, the\ntraps would tell them. If not, they would go down in the morning.</p>\n<p>Lily and Marigold went out for materials and came back again and again with armloads of dead branches\nand kindling, and pine boughs, which burn well. Már set to work on a pit in the mouth of the cave,\nset sharpened stakes in the bottom of it, and laid pine branches over the top. They piled kindling\nbeside it so that a torch would set it all ablaze. Makheneb asked whether they could make fire-arrows\nso that no one would need to run in with a torch, and it was easily done: a rag, a little of Lily's\nliquor, and the lantern.</p>\n<p>While he waited, Makheneb felt out the thing below them. Evil has flavours, as spices do. To someone\nwho cannot bear spice, all of it is simply hot; to someone who has grown used to it, one heat is quite\nunlike another. This place had a deep foreboding in it, but it was different from Dol Guldur. It\nintrigued him. He did not feel better prepared for it. He felt drawn. He had found the trail, and\nthis was the place. However frightening that was, this was where he wanted to be, and this was the\nthing he wanted to hunt.</p>\n<p>They waited on the hillside downwind of the cave, near enough to strike quickly. Lily went up to check\non the horses. They would rather have been somewhere warmer, but they were keeping close together for\nthe heat, and they were content.</p>\n<p>The night passed. The wolves did not come back.</p>\n<h2>Down</h2>\n<p>The morning was bright and cold, the kind of cold that has a crystal quality in the sunshine. There\nwas no hot breakfast.</p>\n<p>They left the horses in the ruins on the hilltop, which was the safest place for them, tied with the\nkind of knot a horse can pull free if it has to. If they went in and did not come out, the horses\nwould not be left to starve. If anything happened to Strawberry, Makheneb said, he was never going\nback; he would take the road west to the Grey Havens instead. Lily kissed every one of the animals on\nthe forehead.</p>\n<p>Then they went down. The way sloped gently and kept on sloping. They kept to the pace of the shortest\nlegs among them and went as quietly as they could. It was like going down a very wide, very slow\nspiral stair with no stairs. It took about an hour.</p>\n<p>At the bottom the passage opened into a chamber so large that their light did not reach its walls.\nLily went back up the passage a couple of hundred feet, to where she could still see the others'\nlights, and there she put out her own lantern and kept watch in the dark, listening back up the way\nthey had come.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the floor was a wheel of stone. It was set into the floor and stood about four feet\nhigh, round, and some eight feet across, and a line of symbols ran round it. Marigold took out the\njournal and compared the two. She found where the phrase began and followed it round.</p>\n<p>Two pieces were missing, like two slices out of a pie, and the gaps broke the phrase. One of them lay\non the ground beside the wheel. Marigold lifted it and fitted it back into its place. The other was\nnot anywhere near.</p>\n<p>Looking closely at the edges, they saw the marks of a chisel. Someone had set out to break the\nwheel on purpose, and not long ago. It had been done within the last year.</p>\n<p>One of them had seen something glint against the far wall as they came in, and turned a lantern\nthat way. There were doors: a pair of them, huge, faced with a skin of metal worked in filigree. The\ndesign was abstract and flowing and graceful, and the style of it was utterly strange to all of them.\nThe doors did not look as though they had been opened in a long time.</p>\n<p>\"That is not Dwarf-work,\" said Már. \"Not any kind.\"</p>\n<p>Somebody asked whether they could open it. Somebody else said, \"Slow down.\" They did not know what\nwas on the other side.</p>\n<p>They went round the walls of the chamber instead, all together, with the lanterns. Near the wall, not\nfar from the doors, they found a skeleton, and it was holding the missing piece.</p>\n<h2>The belt</h2>\n<p>It had been dead a long while; there was nothing left of it but bones. Nothing had eaten it. It had\nnot been mauled. It looked human, and its clothes had not rotted at all. There was a weapon beside it,\nand some other things.</p>\n<p>Perry looked at it without touching it, and remembered.</p>\n<p>A year and a half ago, perhaps two, there had been a very shady-looking man outside Bree, asking a\ngreat many questions. Perry had pulled him aside at the gate and was thinking of taking it further,\nand then Perry's superior had come over and said, <em>I'll take care of this.</em> When Perry followed it up\nafterwards, the answers he got did not make sense. They implied that nothing had really happened, and\nthat the man had gone on his way without hindrance. Perry had never had a good opinion of the man he\nanswered to at the gate, though he was not one to hold a poor opinion of anybody, and he had kept it\ndown. He supposed his superior must have his reasons.</p>\n<p>That man had worn a braided belt of blue and white. Perry had never seen another like it. And here it\nwas, on the skeleton.</p>\n<p>\"This is him,\" said Perry. \"Do you remember, a year and a half back? This man asking all sorts of\nquestions outside town?\"</p>\n<p>Lily had heard about it from some of the farmsteads. It had become something of an issue.</p>\n<p>As Perry described him, the man took shape before them in the air: a shadowy form, in the likeness of\nthe man he remembered.</p>\n<p>Every lantern went out.</p>\n<h2>The ghost</h2>\n<p>Up the passage, Lily had let her eyes grow used to the dark. She had been watching the others' lights,\nand all at once the lights went out, every one of them. She called out: \"What's going on over there?\"</p>\n<p>What came back was shouting and shrieking.</p>\n<p>In the chamber it was black. There was a thing in the dark with them, and they could not see it. Már\nknew the dead as well as anyone living, and this was not like the dead she knew. She was a long way\nfrom home ground. But she knew one thing about it. It had put out the lanterns because it hated light\nand fire. That was a thing they might use.</p>\n<p>\"Follow my voice,\" someone called. \"Come to me. We'll get together so we can feel we're together, and\nput our backs to each other.\"</p>\n<p>They groped their way together. Makheneb struck his lantern alight again. The light blinded him for a\nmoment, and when his eyes opened the ghost was right in front of him. Its hand closed over the flame,\nand as it drew back the flame went out again. But he had seen it, if only for an instant, and he had\nan arrow ready. The arrow passed through it with an unnatural, shrill sound, and then it was dark\nagain.</p>\n<p>Már set herself to guard the lantern that was left. And when the light came again and Már saw the thing in it, she had had enough. She swung her axe with all\nher strength behind it and cleaved through the ghost. They heard the crunch of the skeleton behind it\nas the blade went on through. There was a howl that seemed to suck the air out of the room, and then\nsilence.</p>\n<p>They lit the lanterns. There was nothing there but the crushed and cloven bones. Everyone stood very\nstill for a long moment.</p>\n<p>Then they took the missing piece from the skeleton's hands, carried it back to the wheel, and fitted\nit in. There were two layers to the stone. The lower one was unharmed, and the piece rested on it\nsecurely, and the carving ran whole round the wheel again.</p>\n<p>As it settled into place, they heard a soft sound. The great doors had opened, a little way, inward.</p>", "played": "2025-05-20", "slug": "02-findemir-and-the-ruins", "title": "Findemir & the Ruins"}, {"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>What the ghost had been</h2>\n<p>The great doors stood open a crack, and nobody went through them at once.</p>\n<p>\"I've seen a few ghosts in my day,\" said Már. \"That one was the most sinister I have ever met.\"</p>\n<p>\"You see why Findemir was bringing you along,\" somebody said. \"You and ghosts, split with an axe.\"</p>\n<p>Perry knew the ghost. He had known the man it had been. \"We tried to bar him from Bree,\" he said, \"and he\nstill got in. He went round by a back way.\"</p>\n<p>What had the man been doing here? He had tried to take a piece of the wheel away, and with a piece\nmissing the doors would never open. Was that what he had wanted, to keep them shut? Or had somebody\nsent him, as somebody had sent them, only to do the opposite? He had not seemed the leading kind to\nPerry. He had seemed like the kind of man someone sends to do their dirty work. And if someone had\nsent him, there might be others.</p>\n<p>They had tramped all over the floor by now, but they looked for footprints anyway, working round the\nroom from the door, past the wheel, and on to the place where the body lay. They could tell their own\nprints from any others: this was Makheneb's, and this was Lily's. Once those were set aside, the dead\nman's movements could be followed through the dust. Following them, they found a ledge in a dark\ncorner of the wall, a little above head height and nowhere near the doors. On it was a Ranger's\npack. Inside was dried food of the kind that keeps in a cool place underground: an emergency store.\nIt was not food for a hard march. It was enough to keep one person from starving for two or three\nweeks, until they could get somewhere better.</p>\n<p>\"A gift from Findemir,\" said somebody. If they were shut in behind those doors, there was food for\none of them for three weeks, or for all five of them for about five days.</p>\n<p>Near the doors they found a second set of prints, not the dead man's. They went round and round the\nwheel of stone, and then they went in through the doors, into the inner chamber. They did not come\nout again. The doors had been opened after all, and not so long ago.</p>\n<p>Már looked at the skeleton. Undead were her trade, and she knew how odd deaths looked. Round its neck\nwas a necklace of Dwarvish make, with a badger worked on it in profile. She did not believe for a\nmoment that the man had come by it honestly. She took it, and wearing it she felt a little more\nformidable than before.</p>\n<p>Then she read the bones as Perry had read the floor. The man had fallen several feet away from the\nwall, and then he had pushed himself backward along the ground until the wall stopped him. He had\nbeen trying to get away from something. She thought he had died of terror.</p>\n<p>\"Did he just need his friend to open the door,\" Lily wondered, \"and then his friend said goodbye?\"</p>\n<h2>The stair</h2>\n<p>The doors were higher than the chamber's own ceiling and half again as wide as a room. They had\nopened inward just a crack, a couple of feet across. There were handles; to swing a door wide would\nhave taken two of them hauling together, and it might have been loud. They slipped through the gap\ninstead.</p>\n<p>Beyond, a wide staircase went down, as wide as the doors. Here were the ruins proper. But nothing had\ncome in here in earnest, and it had been sealed for most of its long life, so it was not ruined at\nall, only old and unattended. The stonework was beautiful. The floor was level and the stairs were\ntrue stairs. Lily badly wanted to touch the stone.</p>\n<p>Should they prop the door? They had nothing to do it with, short of the skeleton, and no one wanted to\ntouch that. In the end they simply went on.</p>\n<p>Going quietly was impossible. They had no light but the lanterns, and the lanterns would draw\nanything that was waiting for them; and stairs are loud. They went down together, the stairs wide\nenough for all of them, with one of them three steps behind to watch the way they had come. The stair\ncurved gently to the left, not a corkscrew but a slow turn, and went down three or four storeys.</p>\n<h2>The chamber</h2>\n<p>At the bottom there were lamps along the walls, ready to be lit. The floor gave off a faint chill.\nIt looked almost crystalline, a pale blue, like frosted glass. It crunched a little underfoot, like\nice, though it did not feel like true ice. The light of their lanterns died away before it reached\nthe ceiling. On the walls were beautiful geometric patterns in tile, of the kind usually set into a\nfloor; a few had fallen, but most were whole.</p>\n<p>\"They'll see us regardless,\" somebody said. \"We might as well be able to see them.\" Perry began\nlighting the lamps as he passed.</p>\n<p>As the lamps caught one after another, the room came out of the dark. It was very large, some two\nhundred and fifty feet across, and nearly eight-sided. The ceiling arched up to a small, shallow dome.\nThere were three side rooms. In the middle was a wheel of stone like the one outside: a wide, flat\nround of stone, but four times as broad. And standing on it was a man.</p>\n<p>He was holding the hilt of a sword. The rest of the blade lay in pieces about his feet. He was bent\nover the stone as though he had been trying to prise it open, or break it, and he was absolutely\nstill.</p>\n<p>He had skin. He was not a skeleton, and he was not quite a statue. He looked like a perfect likeness\nof himself, in all his colours, only drained of them and turned dull and drab. There was no\nbreath and no movement in him. Marigold touched him. His skin was cold, like cold stone, and yet\nthere was still some give to it. He had not been turned to stone. He had simply stopped.</p>\n<p>The sight of him chilled them all.</p>\n<p>The sword had been a long sword. \"That's a noble's weapon,\" somebody said. \"It's not what the other\none would carry.\" Perhaps this was the man who had hired him.</p>\n<p>Perry looked at the hilt. It bore a bird of prey, an eagle perhaps. He had seen something like it once\nbefore, though not the same. A Ranger who came through Bree had carried a sword with a hilt of that\nkind, and had said it was an heirloom of his forefathers, and that the first pieces of it went back\nto the Second Age, thousands of years ago. This was not that sword, but it came from the same family\nof things. It was as old as this place.</p>\n<p>The man had been trying to drive the blade into the stone like a wedge. There was hardly a seam to\ndrive it into, only a tiny crack.</p>\n<p>The Bree-folk had seen enough people come through their town to judge him. He was a Man, and they\nguessed he came from further south than Tharbad. He was no Ranger.</p>\n<h2>The side rooms</h2>\n<p>Makheneb went round the three side rooms while the others stood by the wheel.</p>\n<p>The first was a small living space, with room for two or three to sleep, a chest, some blankets left\nbehind, a table, and a whetstone. Anyone who needed to stay here for a while could.</p>\n<p>The second was for reading and writing. It had a rough wooden chair and a table, slanted shelves for\nscrolls, and a shelf and a half of books bound in leather. The scroll-shelves were two-thirds full.\nThere was a jar of ink. It was a spartan room; the books and scrolls were by far the most valuable\nthings in it.</p>\n<p>The third was a store. Spare furniture for the other rooms was kept there, and shelves of things\nwrapped in cloths and leather. Makheneb unwrapped some of them. They were relics, extremely old, some\nof precious metal and some of exquisite craft. At the back were a few weapons and some armour, of\nstrange style and interesting workmanship.</p>\n<p>Before he said a word to anyone, he went over it for any taint. He was more learned in the Shadow than\nthe others. None of it felt like the two dead men. The things in this room looked as though they had\nalways been here, and were meant to be. There was also a bow there, a very fine one. He took it.</p>\n<p>Then he came back and told the others that there was treasure in there, some kind of armoury, and he\nwas not sure it was theirs to take. Perhaps it was the sort of place where you take a bowl and leave a\nbowl.</p>\n<h2>The wheel, and what was under the floor</h2>\n<p>Marigold compared the carving on the great wheel with the journal. It matched perfectly. She did not\nknow the language, but she had heard of protective charms, and of wards, and of words that seal\nsomething shut. She thought this might be one. If so, what mattered was that the words were whole.\nThey were whole. They were here to check on things, and this checked out.</p>\n<p>Lily was all for leaving the frozen man. Moriel had told them to check the symbols, not to rescue\npeople who had been caught trying to break them. But nobody liked the thought of leaving somebody who\nmight still be alive.</p>\n<p>While they talked, Marigold saw something. A faint vapour, almost too slight to see, was rising from\nthe crack in the stone where the man had been prying. She felt a slight chill from it before it\nthinned away. \"We should get him off that,\" she said.</p>\n<p>Then Perry and Lily looked down.</p>\n<p>The floor under their feet was beautiful, and they had thought it opaque. Something shifted, and they\nsaw that it was not as opaque as they had thought. The whole floor was a window. Under it was a beast.\nIts back had been pressed up against the underside of the glass, and now it rolled over.</p>\n<p>They saw it in profile. It was a couple of hundred feet long. It had fur like a bear's, white, and\nhuge horns and tusks. It was shaped more like a man than a dragon; if they had known of such a thing,\nthey would have thought of an ape of monstrous size. It was asleep. It had only rolled over in its\nsleep. None of them had heard, even in a fairy-story, of anything like it.</p>\n<p>The dread of it went through all of them. Then it settled, and it did not move again.</p>\n<h2>The ledger</h2>\n<p>Nobody wanted to stay long after that. But the journal said that the one who came to check should add\nan entry to the log, and Marigold meant to do it properly.</p>\n<p>They tiptoed into the reading room. The book lying readiest to hand was the log. Every few decades\nthere was a new entry, and most of them were perfunctory: the date, who had come, that they had checked\nthe things they were meant to check and stayed three days, and that all was well. Sometimes they added\nsomething else they had done or noticed in the country round about. Year after year after year, it\nwent back. Even the recent entries were written in a kind of shorthand that Marigold guessed belonged\nto the Rangers. Going further back, the writing grew harder and harder to read, until a couple of\ncenturies back she could not read it at all.</p>\n<p>There was nothing in it about a monster. There was nothing about a frozen man.</p>\n<p>It was, Marigold thought, like the note on the wall of a privy that says who cleaned it last and\nwhether the soap had been filled. And now here was she: <em>Marigold was here</em>, not looking at the\nmonster the whole time.</p>\n<p>She wrote the date and a full account of what they had found. In the margins she described the sleeping\nbeast. Then, with a good deal of skill, she drew it. The head and the face came out especially well,\nand she caught the shape of the rest. It took her the best part of an hour.</p>\n<p>Lily read what she could meanwhile. The scrolls were in a different, older language, in a much more\nflowing hand. Even in the books she could not read, the years were numbered in a way she could follow,\nand she could see that some of the earliest were five hundred or seven hundred years old. The entries\nvaried, too. In one book an entry ran to eight or ten pages, and the next ran to a page and a half, and\nthe next to half a page. That long one was from about a hundred and fifty years ago. Someone, then, had\nmet with something. Lily could not read what.</p>\n<p>After three-quarters of an hour Lily was frightened and fascinated at once, and after an hour she was\nmaking frantic gestures at the others to wrap it up. Was the thing asleep for the winter, or only\ntaking a nap? What had done this to the man? She did not like that he looked petrified and not dead,\nwith that underneath him, and she wanted to be a long way from the floor.</p>\n<h2>The armoury</h2>\n<p>Perry went to the storeroom to see whether there was anything in it that might help them if the beast\ngot loose. There were some daggers. There was a gap where a sword had been, and Perry guessed it was\nthe sword the frozen man had broken. There was another gap where a bow had been — the one Makheneb had\ntaken. There were a great many arrows and arrowheads, a halberd, and some breastplates, ornamental ones\nby the look of them, with crests none of them knew.</p>\n<p>A breastplate sounded good to Perry. Steel had to be better than leather. He put one on in place of his\ncorslet. It was made for a man a good foot taller than he was, but not so much larger that he could not\nwear it.</p>\n<p>The arrowheads looked new and fit for use, not decoration. Makheneb did not trust the old shafts, but\nthe heads were good, and he could fletch new arrows easily enough. Arrowheads were not really treasure,\nhe reasoned. He took some, and dropped a few more where the others could pick them up.</p>\n<p>Már went to the store as well, to see whether there was any sign of the dead in it. There was none;\nbut there were the shelves. There were candelabra, and goblets, and pottery that had kept its shape\nthough its colours had faded, all of it exquisite, and some of the craftsmanship was new even to a\nDwarf. She meant to take only a little, and she took more. She took two goblets, a ring worked with a\nbird, and a band of gold for her hair set with very tasteful gems. It would look good on her.</p>\n<p>Makheneb gathered up the shards of the broken sword and wrapped them in a cloth, and worked the hilt out\nof the frozen man's hands. The hands were firm enough that the hilt came away on its own. It might be\nreforged somewhere.</p>\n<h2>The frozen man</h2>\n<p>In the end they would not leave him. It would be wrong to leave a man who might be alive to die down\nthere. And if he knew something, it might be worth a great deal.</p>\n<p>They tied him to a cot from the sleeping room, for they did not trust him not to wake up possessed, and\na couple of them carried him. His limbs could be moved, they found, but they did not dare try. Perry put\nout every lamp he had lit. They carried the man up the stairs and out into the outer chamber, and pushed\nthe great doors shut behind them. There was no click, but the doors felt secure.</p>\n<p>Far away up the passage, the way they had come, something whined, high and thin. Then something howled.</p>\n<p>Marigold felt a cold place in the room. Past the stone wheel, near the skeleton, there was a lump on the\nfloor that had not been there when they went in. It looked like a lump of ice, and it was changing. As\nshe watched, a paw came out of it, and then another, not quite finished. It was making itself out of\nthe ice.</p>\n<p>\"We have to go,\" she said. \"We have to go.\"</p>\n<p>\"Light me an arrow,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>They went on up with the frozen man between them, and Makheneb walked backwards with his bow, waiting.\nThe thing's face began to form. It was the face of one of the wolves they had fought. He shot it through\nthe head with a burning arrow just as it found its shape. The fire went on burning inside it, and it\nburned from the inside out. It had not even formed well enough to howl. It gave a pale whimper, and fell\nin on itself, and melted away.</p>\n<p>At the top of the long slope the mouth of the cave showed against the evening light. There was a wolf\nthere, one only, looking in and down. In the pit, something was still moaning.</p>\n<p>They charged it all together, and Már led them. She was carrying the frozen man's feet, and she let\nthem go and bellowed and went forward, staring at the wolf with the badger at her throat. She was the\nbigger dog. The wolf froze where it stood and met her eyes. Then it yelped, and turned, and ran.</p>\n<p>They went round the pit. The wolf that had fallen in was dead now on the stakes. As they came out of the\ncave they could see, far off on the other side, three wolves running away over the horizon.</p>\n<h2>By night</h2>\n<p>They went up to the ruins for the horses. They had four mounts for the five of them and the frozen\nman, and they doubled up; the frozen man rode on Leaper in front of Perry. They would not reach Barad\nTarminalë before dark. It would be three or four hours after nightfall. They decided to push on, since\nthere would be a good rest at the end of it.</p>\n<p>On the way they stopped for an hour to see whether the man could be woken. Somebody felt for a pulse.\nThere was none, and no breath. Nothing that had happened, not even all the jolting, had stirred him.\nLily brought out her healing balm.</p>\n<p>Makheneb waited for nightfall. Then he laid the man down on a bed of leaves and tried the ways of his\nown people, the Elvish ways, and set himself to drive out whatever darkness had come into him. The\nhealing did nothing. But as he worked, a realisation came over him. He had dealt with things born of\nthe evil of Dol Guldur, and things near to it. This came from an evil as great as that, or greater, and\nthere was nothing in him that could undo it. The man was not dead. But Makheneb knew of no way to bring\nhim back.</p>\n<p>\"This is some bad magic,\" he told the others.</p>\n<p>They searched the man's pockets. There were no marks on him. Inside one pocket they found a slip of paper\nrolled up and tied to the lining, so that it could not be lost. When they unrolled it there was a\npassphrase written on it, like the call or the answer that gets you in at a locked door.</p>\n<h2>Ghostcleavers</h2>\n<p>They came to Barad Tarminalë in the dark, and rested there.</p>\n<p>They called for Tom, as Tom had told them they might in need, but Tom did not answer.</p>\n<p>In the days of rest that followed, they made a song of victory for Már. They called it <em>Ghostcleavers</em>.</p>\n<p>About five days later, Marigold and Lily woke early, as they always did, and found Tom Bombadil in the\nkitchen cooking breakfast.</p>\n<p>Tom looked at the frozen man. He said plainly that he could not undo what had been done to him.</p>\n<p>Then Marigold showed him her drawing of the white beast under the floor, and Tom changed. He knew it.\nThere were several of these beings, he told them, bound or sleeping in secret places. He took Perry's\nmap, and on it he marked where others lay: in the North Moors, near the Shire; south of Tharbad; and in\nthe Blue Mountains.</p>\n<h2>The road west</h2>\n<p>When the Company set out west, they fell in with another company on the road, a curious and peaceful\none. There was Celenneth, a Ranger of the Dúnedain, and Linnea, a woman of Bree, who was her partner;\ntheir children, Brynja, the older girl, and Hallas, who was still small; and with them Eryndil, a\nscholarly Elf of Lindon.</p>\n<p>The two companies travelled together for a time under the early summer sky, and told each other their\nstories, and what they meant to do.</p>", "played": "2025-06-03", "slug": "03-the-inner-chamber", "title": "The Inner Chamber"}], "company": [], "home": {"html": "<h1>Banes of Beleriand</h1>"}, "people": [], "timeline": {"html": "<h1>Timeline</h1>"}};
